The original does a linear scan over the whole message table on every call. This version builds a one-time direct-index table keyed by message number.

- **Lookup cost:** each call becomes a single O(1) array read.
- **Same result as before:** the table is filled from the highest index down, so when two entries share a number the lowest index wins. That is the same entry the linear scan returns first.
- **Behavior otherwise unchanged:** numbers with no entry still return `NULL`.
- **Initialization:** the table depends only on the constant `mesgs` array. It is built once through a function-local static, which C++ initializes thread-safely.
- **Assumption:** the scheme relies on `MESGS` being well below `0xFFFF`, since that value marks "no entry".
- **Memory:** the table uses 128 KB.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    static const std::unique_ptr<FIT_UINT16[]> table = []()
    {
        std::unique_ptr<FIT_UINT16[]> t(new FIT_UINT16[65536]);
        std::fill(t.get(), t.get() + 65536, (FIT_UINT16)0xFFFF);
        for (int i = (int)MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
            t[mesgs[i].num] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
        return t;
    }();

    const FIT_UINT16 idx = table[num];
    if (idx == 0xFFFF)
        return NULL;
    return &mesgs[idx];
}
```

```includes
#include <memory>
#include <algorithm>
```